--TEST--
A column letter is upper case, `A` to `XFD`, and any other text is a key: a key before the first row with keys throws naming the key, a letter works before any row, a lower-case letter is a key, and a key of the `columns` option works before any row: rule:core-classes/spreadsheet-roster
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Novis\Spreadsheet\Sheet::cell
use Novis\Spreadsheet\Workbook;

var $book = Workbook::createInMemory();
var $orders = $book->sheet("Orders");
try {
    echo $orders->cell("qty", 2), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// A column letter needs no row.
echo $orders->cell("C", 2), "\n";

// The first row with keys gives the keys their columns.
$orders->add(["order" => 1042, "qty" => 3]);
echo $orders->cell("qty", 2), "\n";

// A lower-case letter is a key, and this sheet has no key `c`.
try {
    echo $orders->cell("c", 2), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $e) {
    echo $e->message, "\n";
}

// The keys of the `columns` option are known before the first row.
var $returns = $book->sheet("Returns", {columns: ["order" => {header: "Order"}, "qty" => {header: "Qty"}]});
echo $returns->cell("qty", 2), "\n";
$returns->add(["order" => 1042, "qty" => 1]);
$book->finish();
--EXPECT--
The sheet `Orders` has no column keys yet, so `qty` is not a column. The keys come from the `columns` option or from the first row with keys.
C2
B2
The sheet `Orders` has no column `c`. Give a key of its columns or an upper-case column letter, such as `C`.
B2
